A number of people have complained about being killed over a wrong
keystroke.  This arises in the context of drowning (current
discussion) and in the context of eyes.  They blame such deaths on the
user interface rather than on poor play.

Hairston writes:
>[] that's a feature ...
>let's not lean too heavily on "is it realistic?". "is it playable?"
>is a real concern.

Nice attempt to warp the discussin, David.  But I won't let you get
away with it.  Is it "fun" or "interesting" is the real concern.  Or
do you think that it is fun to die by drowning?  Is it fun the second
time?  The third?  Just how fun is it for you?  Or do you just think
it is fun when *other* people die this way?

The simple solution is to request confirmation before actions that are
clear mistakes.  At least make it an option.  Are you really "giving
something away" by allowing an option that requests confirmation
before entering pools of water?  I see that it would be giving
something away if there was an option for floating eyes; that is why I
have suggested that the option take a list of monsters.  No spoiler
there, and a lot more useful.  Although Hairston has repeatedly denounced
this suggestion, I have never had a response from anyone on the dev team.

I am at a loss to explain why the dev team repeatedly ignores this
kind of request.  Are these things too hard to implement?  (I
seriously doubt it.)  Perhaps the dev team likes it when players die,
too.  Or maybe they think that all is fair under the HARD option.  I
don't think so.  Perhaps an ALLSFAIR option or, more accurately, an
OBNOXIOUS option is called for.

Now, on to David's question: what to do if someone really wants to
walk into the water.  He should have a chance to reach the other side
based on dex, strength, and amount of weight of stuff carried.  This
would be interesting and fun, though probably a lot of work for a
piece of code that most players would never execute.  Several turns
should be allowed, during which lots of hit points are lost and
possibly some timeout as for tainted meat so that a guy with 2000 HP
can't stay down for 20 minutes.  The multiple turns would allow
multiple attempts to teleport, time to remove and drop heavy items,
and multiple chances to climb out the other side.  Ideally, praying
should be implemented though talking under water might lower your odds
of pronouncing your prayer correctly.  Of course most of your stuff
would be damaged or destroyed.

And one other thing: Confirmation should consist of more than just
typing y (yes<cr> is reasonable).  Perhaps the dev team has never
encountered a sticky y key or a modem that occasionally sends
incorrect characters.  Or just the poor guy who tries to quaff potion
y after accidentally depressing the caps lock.  Is this a feature,
too?

My apologies for being so caustic in public.  The dev team's repeated
failure to even respond to complaints about basic misfeatures just
gets to me.

